## 1. Forced seven-row shadow

The preceding eight-no-root `k=6` reduction has already forced the global `D=-2,p=8` branch to

```text
rank(P_8)=2,
d_v=2,
q=8,
eight singleton changed blocks.
```

It also proves that the rank-two coefficient space has at least two three-clean coordinates. Fix one such coordinate `i`. Deleting `i` gives a seven-row state `X_7` with

```text
rank(P_7)=2,
D_7=0,
p_7=8,
d_7=2,
q_7>=7,
singleton capacity >=7.
```

Because `i` is three-clean, deleting any further one or two coordinates still preserves coefficient rank two. Thus every six-row puncture of `X_7` is synchronized, and every five-row puncture obtained from it still has rank-two coefficient space.

The preceding maximal-functional-rank reduction applies to `X_7`. Its canonical functional quotient has dimension

```text
(7-2)+2 = 7.
```

The defect bound

```text
D >= t + p + 3d - 3s
```

forces `s_7=7`. Therefore the actual singleton replacement functionals in `X_7` span the entire seven-dimensional canonical functional quotient.

The point of this note is that full quotient span survives puncturing.

## 2. Puncture functoriality of the canonical functional quotient

Consider a virtual-canonical state on `t` no-root rows with coefficient-constraint space

```text
P <= Q^t,
rank(P)=r.
```

Let `C` be its virtual-complete root-block set, with `c=|C|`, and let

```text
N = null(P),
dim(N)=t-r.
```

Assume the established external-containment property: every canonical external relation from `N` lies in the span of the complete-block relations. Let `d` be the complete-row deficiency. The canonical relation matrix has rows indexed by

```text
C union B_N,
```

where `B_N` is a basis of `N`. Its rank is `c-d`, so the row-index functional quotient has dimension

```text
(t-r)+d.
```

Now delete a coordinate `j`, assuming the punctured coefficient space still has rank `r`. Write the punctured state with primes. Then

```text
N' = {alpha in N : alpha_j=0}
```

has codimension one in `N`. Choose a basis `B_N'` of `N'` and extend it by one vector to a basis `B_N` of `N`.

The virtual-complete sets satisfy

```text
C' subset C.
```

For `alpha in N'`, the parent canonical external relation is exactly the punctured canonical external relation after the row-index coordinates outside `C' union B_N'` are removed. The only blocks requiring comment are those in `C\C'`:

- if such a block becomes untouched after deleting `j`, its local demand is absent;
- if it remains touched but becomes virtual-empty, its local quotient demand vanishes on `N'` by the definition of virtual emptiness.

In either case the parent complete-state correction on `alpha` is zero, so no extra term remains when passing to the puncture.

Let `M` and `M'` be the parent and punctured canonical relation matrices, regarded as matrices whose columns lie in their respective row-index spaces. Coordinate restriction

```text
rho : Q^(C union B_N) -> Q^(C' union B_N')
```

therefore satisfies

```text
rho(Col(M)) = Col(M').
```

Consequently `rho` induces a surjection of functional quotients

```text
bar(rho): Q^(C union B_N)/Col(M)
          -> Q^(C' union B_N')/Col(M').          (2.1)
```

### Replacement functionals under puncturing

Let `b in C` be changed from the virtual-complete state to a compatible incomplete state, and let `ell_b` be its replacement functional.

If `b in C'`, then restriction to `C' union B_N'` gives exactly the punctured replacement functional `ell_b'`: on every `alpha in N'` the local quotient demand and the complete/incomplete coefficient comparison are unchanged.

If `b notin C'`, then the block coordinate itself is removed. On every `alpha in N'` its local demand is zero in the puncture, so both the complete state and the incomplete state have zero coefficient correction. Hence all remaining nullspace coordinates of `ell_b` vanish, and

```text
bar(rho)([ell_b]) = 0.
```

Thus

```text
bar(rho)([ell_b]) = [ell_b']  if b remains virtual-complete,
                    0         otherwise.          (2.2)
```

Equations (2.1) and (2.2) give the required descent principle:

> If a collection of actual replacement functionals spans the full canonical functional quotient before a rank-preserving puncture, then the surviving replacement functionals span the full canonical functional quotient after puncturing.

This statement is basis-independent; the adapted nullspace bases above only make the quotient map explicit.

## 3. First descent forces every six-row deficiency to be two

Delete any coordinate `j` from `X_7`. The resulting six-row coefficient plane still has rank two, and every one-coordinate puncture of it also has rank two. Let its virtual-complete deficiency be `d_6`.

Because its virtual-complete set is contained in the seven-row set, row-nullity monotonicity gives

```text
d_6 <= d_7 = 2.
```

The established synchronized six-row rank-two containment theorem gives

```text
d_6 >= 1.
```

Hence `d_6` is either one or two.

The puncture descent principle applies to the seven-row full-span singleton functionals. Therefore the surviving singleton replacement functionals span the entire six-row quotient, whose dimension is

```text
6-2+d_6 = 4+d_6.
```

If `d_6=1`, at least five surviving singleton replacement functionals are required. But the committed exact synchronized six-row deficiency-one census proves that every such state has compatible singleton capacity at most three:

```text
capacity census = {0:1, 2:6, 3:24}.
```

So `d_6=1` is impossible. Every six-row puncture of `X_7` must therefore satisfy

```text
d_6=2
```

and, because its quotient has dimension six, must have singleton capacity at least six.

## 4. Second descent forces every five-row deficiency to be two

Fix one of those six-row punctures and delete any one of its six coordinates. By three-cleanliness of the original deleted coordinate, the resulting five-row coefficient space still has rank two.

Let its virtual-complete deficiency be `d_5`. The complete set only shrinks, so

```text
d_5 <= d_6 = 2.
```

The repaired five-row rank-two containment theorem gives

```text
d_5 >= 1.
```

The six-row replacement functionals already span the full six-row quotient by Section 3. Applying the puncture descent principle again, the surviving singleton functionals span the entire five-row quotient, of dimension

```text
5-2+d_5 = 3+d_5.
```

If `d_5=1`, at least four compatible singleton blocks are required. The committed exact five-row deficiency-one census has singleton capacity at most three. Therefore `d_5=1` is impossible, and every such five-row puncture has

```text
d_5=2.
```

We have proved that every hypothetical `X_7` produces, after deleting any one of its coordinates, a synchronized six-row rank-two state satisfying all of the following:

1. virtual deficiency `d_6=2`;
2. singleton capacity at least six;
3. all six one-coordinate five-row projections have virtual deficiency `d_5=2`.

## 5. The pre-existing exact audit has zero such six-row states

The exact verifier

`research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_six_noroot_k4_d2_singleton_capacity_2026_09_21.py`

already exhausts precisely this structural class. Its full census contains

```text
synchronized six-row d_6=2 states                  1,418
with singleton capacity >=6                          761
with all six projected five-row deficiencies d_5=2     0
```

The verifier works from the exact `S_7` five-row orbit cover, enumerates every realizable rank-two six-row extension, requires all six coordinate projections to retain rank two, and uses exact rational coefficient-space operations plus Hadamard-certified modular complete-row ranks.

Therefore the six-row state forced by Sections 3 and 4 does not exist. This contradicts the hypothetical seven-row shadow `X_7`.

Hence the global eight-row branch

```text
D=-2,
k=6,
d_root=0,
p=8
```

is impossible.

## 6. Consequence for the current eight-no-root `k=6` layer

The earlier reduction had already closed five of the seven initial arithmetic branches and reduced the `D=-2` side to the single profile closed above. Therefore there is now no eight-no-root `k=6` counterexample with `D=-2`.

The remaining eight-no-root `k=6` work is entirely on

```text
D=-1,
d_root=0,
p=9.
```

Before this note its surviving virtual profiles were

```text
(d_v,q,singleton_min) =
(1,7,5), (1,8,7),
(2,8,7), (2,9,9), (3,9,9).
```

The maximal-functional-rank theorem additionally requires these profiles to fill quotient ranks

```text
7, 7, 8, 8, 9
```

respectively. Those are now the next closure targets.
